      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Published by the BMJ Publishing Group Limited. For permission to use (where not already granted under a licence) please go to http://www.bmj.com/company/products-services/rights-and-licensing/.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Age and body mass index (BMI) are positively associated with the development of the metabolic syndrome (MetS). Cardiorespiratory fitness (CRF) can attenuate BMI-related increases in prevalence of MetS, but the nature of this association across different age strata has not been fully investigated."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "AIM",
            "content": "To identify the association between CRF and MetS prevalence across age strata (20-69\u2005years) and determine whether associations are independent of BMI."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "CRF was estimated from incremental treadmill exercise in British men attending preventative health screening. Participants were placed in four age strata (20-39, 40-49, 50-59 and 60-69\u2005years) and classified as fit or unfit using age-related cut-offs. The presence of MetS was defined using the National Cholesterol Education Program Adult Treatment Panel III criteria."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "9666 asymptomatic men (48.7±8.4\u2005years) were enrolled. The prevalence of MetS was 25.5% in all men and ranged from 17.1% in those aged 20-39\u2005years to 30.6% in those aged 60-69\u2005years. Fit men's likelihood of meeting the criteria for MetS was half (OR=0.51, 95% CI 0.46 to 0.57) that of unfit men. The likelihood of MetS was 32-53% lower across age strata in fit, compared with unfit men. Adjustment for BMI attenuated the association, though it remained significant in men aged 20-49\u2005years."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "The cardiometabolic benefits of CRF are independent of BMI particularly in men <50\u2005years. Public health messages should emphasise the important role of CRF alongside weight management for enhancing cardiometabolic health."
          }
        ]
      },
